@@ -61,11 +61,9 @@ Checked on a Mac (M1, macOS 27, headless, an island save) against 16:9: the 3D v
 
 ## iPhone portrait (current)
 
-A phone standing up shows the game's 16:9 picture above the touch controls, not a portrait picture: `dm/ios/Sources/DmLayout.h` gives the picture the height its own width asks for, puts it at the safe area's top and gives the rest of the window to the band under it, where `DmGamepad.m`'s portrait anchors draw the pad (the two thumb groups at 0.28 and 0.72 of the width, 0.58 down) over the companion's own view (below). A 390x844 phone (iPhone 14 and the like) gets a 390x219 picture with no bars above or below it and a 390x566 band; the safe areas are kept whole, so the notch and the home indicator are clear of both. Halving that window instead left the picture 219 of the upper part's 422 points, bars making up the other 48 % of it. The picture never takes more than half the window: an iPad's 4:3 asks for more than half, so it keeps the midpoint split it had, and an iPad mini's 1.52 picture is its own height either way, with only the bars it used to have going to the band. A fold sets both edges itself.
+A phone standing up shows the game's picture in the upper half of the window and the companion band in the lower half: `dm/ios/Sources/DmLayout.h` splits the window at its midpoint, and the picture is fitted into that half. *Picture shape* `auto` asks for the half's own shape (twice the window's width / its height: 0.92 on a 19.5:9 phone, 1.12 on a 375x667 one, 1.39 on an iPad), so the fitted picture fills the half exactly: its background covers the area with nothing cropped and nothing stretched. The game's own layouts keep 16:9, centred in the picture (the adapter's `SetProjectionMtx` y-row division, below), so its HUD and menus are not stretched either; the touch screen maps a touch on the taller picture back onto that centred rectangle (`ui_y` in `hle_touch.c`), so a control is hit where it is drawn. A manual `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` that does not match the half keeps its whole picture and leaves bars instead of cropping it. The picture never takes more than half the window, so the band keeps the other half; a fold sets both edges itself.
 
-The picture keeps 16:9 because a phone's long side is 1.78 to 2.16 times its short one, and 16:9 across the full width is what leaves the band the room a phone standing up has. The front end therefore resolves *Picture shape* `auto` to `0` (16:9) for a window taller than it is wide whose long side is 1.77 or more, and keeps the window's own shape for an iPad or an unfolded Duo (1.33 to 1.52), where the picture is taller than 16:9.
-
-**`auto` is resolved once, at the game's start**, because `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` is written into the frame step state page and the game builds its window textures from it. A game started in portrait keeps its 16:9 picture after the phone turns to landscape (with black bars at the sides); to get the wider picture of [Screen shape](#screen-shape-dm_screen_aspect-current) on a 19.5:9 phone, start in landscape. Turning a landscape game to portrait keeps the wide picture, fitted into the upper part.
+**`auto` is resolved once, at the game's start**, because `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` is written into the frame step state page and the game builds its window textures from it. A game started in portrait keeps its 0.92 picture after the phone turns to landscape (letterboxed at the sides); to get the wider picture of [Screen shape](#screen-shape-dm_screen_aspect-current) on a 19.5:9 phone, start in landscape. Turning a landscape game to portrait keeps the wide picture, fitted into the upper half.
 
 **A phone standing up is handheld, so the game's own keyboard takes touches.** The profile sets no `op_mode`, so the game starts docked and the host hands it no touch points (`touch_read` in `hle_hid.c`), which leaves the game's keyboard unreachable. The front end therefore switches the console mode by the screen's orientation while no TV shows the picture (`followOrientation` in `front_ios.m`): standing up it goes handheld and lying down it returns to the mode the player had, a choice made in the in-game menu included. The game reads touches in both modes, so nothing else has to change, and the switch is the console mode change notification the game watches, so it follows while it runs. Handheld also renders at 1280x720, so the picture is the handheld one while the phone stands up. *Console mode* in the in-game menu (or `DM_OPMODE=0` in `Documents/env.txt`) still works for a phone lying down.
 
@@ -89,7 +87,7 @@ On a taller picture the game caches projections and screen rectangles that a cha
 
 `main+0x265140` is the single `ret` of `nn::ui2d::DrawInfo::SetProjectionMtx`. One chained stub runs the upstream y-row division first (its 16:9 test reads m0 and m5, which the adapter's `Lscreen_projection` rewrites), then calls the adapter's keyboard dock; the adapter adds its offset to index 6 instead of overwriting it. `main+0x27d4c0` forces the footer subtree dirty at `Pane::Calculate` entry and `main+0x27d570` runs `duo_calc` after `CalculateGlobalMatrix`: the two were disassembled and coexist.
 
-The adapter follows the host's `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` (1.3 to 1.77) through `dm_fps_state +0x58`; it does not support arbitrary ratios or folding-display rotation. At `+0x58 = 0` (16:9, or `auto` on iOS) every adapter stub runs the original instructions.
+The adapter follows the host's `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` through `dm_fps_state +0x58`. It was built and checked for 1.3 to 1.77; a phone standing up now asks for its upper half's shape (0.92 on a 19.5:9 phone, 1.12 to 1.39 on others), outside that range, and no device has confirmed those ratios yet (an earlier report of a crash at 0.92 has no stack or cause). It does not support arbitrary ratios or folding-display rotation. At `+0x58 = 0` (16:9) every adapter stub runs the original instructions.
 
 The frame stamps are values in the free part of the state page, with no host pointer: `+0xa8` is the host's present counter (`dm_guest_frame_tick`, `hle_os.c`, one relaxed store each present), `+0xb0` the keyboard frame's stamp and `+0xb8` a touch panel's, written by `keyboard_frame` and `touch_page` through `Ltouch_stamp` (`x17` = 8 and 16). `Lrecent_keyboard` subtracts the first two, so "the inline keyboard rendered in the last two presents" needs no host state. `keyboard_window` keeps its own stamp at `+0x38`, which nothing reads: AnimalDuo's touch-context (bit 3) was not ported.
 
diff --git a/dm/host/hle_touch.c b/dm/host/hle_touch.c
index cdabc629..c73783bc 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_touch.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_touch.c
@@ -17,10 +17,11 @@ static struct { int down, pend; float x, y; } g_gf[16];   // the gesture recogni
 static pthread_mutex_t g_tl = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
 static int64_t g_tsamp; static uint64_t g_tlast_ns;
 
-// On a picture wider than 16:9 the game's interface keeps a 16:9 projection (view.s's Lscreen_projection scales the layout projection's x by
-// (16/9)/r), so it is drawn centred across that fraction of the width while the picture itself fills the screen. The front end normalises a
-// touch on the whole picture, so map it back onto that centred rectangle: a control is hit where it is drawn, and the screen's edges reach the
-// interface's edges (a touch outside it clamps there). 16:9 and taller pictures are left alone.
+// On a picture that is not 16:9 the game's interface keeps a 16:9 projection (view.s's Lscreen_projection keeps one axis and scales the other),
+// so it is drawn centred in a 16:9 rectangle while the picture itself fills the screen. The front end normalises a touch on the whole picture, so
+// map it back onto that centred rectangle: a control is hit where it is drawn, and the screen's edges reach the interface's edges (a touch outside
+// it clamps there). A wider picture centres the interface across a fraction of the width, so x maps; a taller one centres it across a fraction of
+// the height, so y maps. 16:9 is left alone.
 __attribute__((weak)) uint8_t *dm_fps_state;   // loader.c defines it; a unit test that links only this file has no frame step page, and NULL means 16:9
 static float ui_x(float x) {
     if (!dm_fps_state) return x;
@@ -29,12 +30,19 @@ static float ui_x(float x) {
     float v = 0.5f + (x - 0.5f) / ((16.0f / 9.0f) / r);
     return v < 0 ? 0 : v > 1 ? 1 : v;
 }
+static float ui_y(float y) {
+    if (!dm_fps_state) return y;
+    float r; memcpy(&r, dm_fps_state + 0x58, 4);
+    if (!(r > 0.0f) || !(r < 16.0f / 9.0f)) return y;
+    float v = 0.5f + (y - 0.5f) * ((16.0f / 9.0f) / r);
+    return v < 0 ? 0 : v > 1 ? 1 : v;
+}
 static void touch_apply(const dm_touch *t, int n) {
     pthread_mutex_lock(&g_tl);
     int seen[16] = {0};
     for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
         uint32_t id = t[i].id; if (id >= 16) continue;
-        float tx = ui_x(t[i].x), ty = t[i].y;
+        float tx = ui_x(t[i].x), ty = ui_y(t[i].y);
         seen[id] = 1;
         if (!g_gf[id].down) g_gf[id].pend = 1;
         g_gf[id].down = 1; g_gf[id].x = tx; g_gf[id].y = ty;
